new file mode 100644
index 0000000..b2b51cf
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/DUO.md
@@ -0,0 +1,49 @@
+# PocketFusion on iPhone Duo
+
+Hold the Duo partly open with the hinge running left to right. **Automatic** mode switches to the handheld layout when iOS reports a suitable fold.
+
+- **Upper screen.** During play, health appears at the upper left, the minimap at the upper right, and the current NPC's portrait and name at the top center. The NPC’s greeting appears here while you talk. Welcome messages, mission updates and other Nanocom notices slide in beneath the minimap. The tracked objective moves down while a notice is visible. Loading artwork and character previews also use this screen.
+- **Lower screen.** Use the left rail to open Chat, Bag, Missions, Map or Menu. Chat history and the input row share the panel to the right of the rail. Login fields, character selection and conversation choices also appear below the fold.
+- **Thumb controls.** Both sticks sit above the midpoint of the lower screen, with MOVE on the left and LOOK farther toward the right edge. Saved custom stick positions take precedence. MOVE works like WASD relative to the camera. Hold LOOK off-center to keep turning and release it to stop. Attack, jump and weapon swap sit within reach of the right thumb, with the other actions below.
+
+## Choose a display mode
+
+Open **Settings → Dual Screen** to open the **PocketFusion display** controls:
+
+| Mode | Behavior |
+| --- | --- |
+| Automatic fold layout | Uses the top-view/bottom-controls layout for a verified, partially open horizontal fold. |
+| Full screen | Keeps the normal game layout. |
+| Handheld layout | Requests the split layout on a verified inner display with usable horizontal division geometry, including when fully open. |
+
+Closing the device, using a vertical book posture, or leaving too little usable space restores the normal layout. Ordinary iPhones retain the single-screen interface. The split layout does not wait for a world connection: it also applies during login, loading and character selection.
+
+## Validation status — November 19, 2026
+
+A Unity render test reproduced the missing lower panels. With the corrected transform, all four corners of a nested panel were visible. Inside taps reached the button, and taps beyond each edge were clipped. Fold geometry checks include the Duo’s reported 669 × 951 points (1878 × 2669 Unity pixels), asymmetric safe areas and transitions into and out of handheld mode.
+
+On the physical Duo, the signed build connected to Retrobution with saved credentials and entered the world while folded. The server list and character selector appeared below the hinge, with the character preview above. Chat showed its history, tabs and input row. Bag showed the avatar, equipment and slots. Missions showed the journal and categories. Map showed Forsaken Valley and its markers. The left rail switched panels, and the map’s Close button restored the lower controls.
+
+Health appeared at the upper left, the minimap at the upper right, and Samurai Jack’s portrait and name at the top center. His greeting appeared above the fold, and Close below returned to play. The larger thumb controls were visible. Manual credential entry with the software keyboard and simultaneous-thumb comfort still need a player check.
+
+An offline Unity check also rendered the full manual Retrobution login form in the lower pane and verified that tapping each text box focused the correct field.
+
+## Controls and rendering update
+
+On the Duo, Mojo Jojo’s welcome appeared below the upper minimap on entering Forsaken Valley. The folded layout checks cover tall banners, tracked objectives, the centered NPC portrait, and touching the upper edge of MOVE.
+
+The HUD text benchmark drew the same 990,000 glyphs over 10,000 warmed passes. Temporary managed allocations for those labels fell from 30.48 MB to zero. Animation sampling uses a shorter search on long ordered tracks. A Mac benchmark of 180,000 lookups on a 256-key track took 1.81 ms, down from 12.44 ms. Unordered tracks retain their forward lookup.
+
+On November 20 the Duo ran both builds at the same Forsaken Valley position and camera angle. After 20 seconds of warmup, each build recorded 60 seconds of idle play with the same graphics settings. Both averaged 30 FPS. Frames over 40 ms fell from 32% to 28%. The 95th-percentile frame interval was 50 ms in both runs, and mean GPU time rose from 6.29 ms to 6.54 ms. Sustained device temperature and battery use were outside this short test.
+
+## Implementation contract
+
+`PocketFold.mm` uses the public iOS 27.1 `UIHingeInteraction` API and the rendering view's reserved division region. It reports normalized geometry and safe-area bounds to `FoldScreenController`; support is based on platform evidence. Unsupported iOS versions and unavailable or ambiguous geometry fall back to full screen.
+
+`FoldScreenController` assigns the upper viewport to the existing world camera. Startup screens use `UiKit.BeginControlCanvas()` to draw and receive input on the lower screen. In-world panels use `BeginPanel()` to reserve space for the left rail. Each panel installs its display transform before defining its clip rectangle in design coordinates, so nested controls draw and receive taps within the same bounds.
+
+`RenderPipelineManager.endContextRendering` restores the full-display viewport for IMGUI. `FoldScreenBackdrop` draws the matte background behind the controls.
+
+Layout changes cancel held actions and reject old fingers until a fresh touch begins. Leaving handheld mode restores the camera's prior viewport. Networking, server validation and the accepted camera-orbit behavior remain shared across layouts.
+
+See [Apple's iPhone Duo preparation guide](https://developer.apple.com/iphone-duo/prepare/) and [UIHingeInteraction documentation](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/uikit/uihingeinteraction) for platform guidance.
