+# Session-owner cleanup checks
+
+Run `python3 tests/SessionOwnerCleanupTest/run.py` with a local .NET 8 SDK
+(`DOTNET_EXE` can select it). The test extracts the production `OnDestroy` bodies
+for WorldBootstrap, CombatSystem, Sfx, NanoModelRt, BulletFx and the Bank / Vendor /
+Trade modal windows into observable lifetime stubs. The production `NumPad.Clear`
+body is exercised as part of modal teardown. The suite passes **62 assertions**.
+It checks that old sessions release their model/cache references, static callbacks
+and owned audio/preview resources, that repeated cleanup is safe, and that
+destroying an older owner preserves a newer owner's singleton/providers.
+
+Modal checks keep each destroyed window's managed shell alive while proving its
+8 MB table/callback payload becomes collectible. They also verify packet events
+are detached before the dispatcher is cleared, pending item actions are discarded
+without executing them, borrowed textures are released without being destroyed,
+and newer windows retain their singleton, overlay and inventory-mode ownership.
+
+The test also checks registration stores the same delegates teardown compares.
+It does not run Unity's scene unload or establish physical-device memory usage;
+repeated logout/login/world entry still requires a device test.
